Benign nocturnal leg cramps (charley horse) without residual daytime neuromuscular weakness
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Sudden nocturnal calf spasm, Palpable taut muscle, Resolution with passive stretch

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-002
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset severe nocturnal leg pain causing extreme distress and awakening from sleep, requiring evaluation for deep vein thrombosis or acute arterial occlusion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden, sharp, localized calf pain interrupting sleep. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to rule out critical vascular emergencies such as DVT or acute limb ischemia prior to the next in-hours period, to prevent potential limb-threatening deterioration.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My calf seized up in the middle of the night and the pain was excruciating.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a sudden onset of intense involuntary muscle contraction in the calf or foot, causing awakening from sleep due to sharp localized pain. Reports spontaneous resolution within minutes after passive stretching. Currently notes a residual mild muscular ache lasting several hours post-cramp. Complete absence of persistent daytime weakness. Lack of fasciculations between episodes.
Functional Status: Independent ambulation, sleep interrupted.
Social/Safety Concerns: Mild fatigue secondary to sleep disruption, fear of recurrent episodes causing anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable at rest, no signs of acute distress.
Physical Exam: Bilateral lower limbs are warm and well-perfused. Normal deep tendon reflexes. Intact lower extremity sensory examination. Visible and palpable taut muscle belly during the episode resolved by time of examination. Diagnostic triad findings confirmed historically and via residual tenderness: sudden nocturnal calf spasm, palpable taut muscle, resolution with passive stretch. Homan's sign negative. Peripheral pulses intact and equal.
Environmental Observations: Bedside clutter cleared, adequate lighting.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No recent initiation of diuretics or statins.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Benign nocturnal leg cramps (charley horse) without residual daytime neuromuscular weakness - Status: Improving
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of unilateral pitting edema, normal calf circumference, and negative Homan's sign.

Acute Arterial Occlusion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Palpable distal pulses, normal capillary refill, and warm extremities.

Peripheral Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact lower extremity sensory examination and normal deep tendon reflexes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced severe, sudden-onset leg pain that could mimic acute vascular pathology. Given the intensity of the pain and the critical need to exclude deep vein thrombosis or acute ischemia, the assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period. A thorough, timely physical examination confirming intact neurovascular status and characteristic muscle spasm resolution prevented unnecessary ED transfer and safely managed the patient in place, strictly meeting urgent assessment criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance. Advice on hydration and gentle calf stretching. Planned non-urgent electrolyte panel including magnesium and potassium.
Positive Behavioral Change: Perform gentle calf stretches against a wall for two minutes before bed to lengthen muscle fibers and prevent nocturnal spasms.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks for blood test results if symptoms persist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if the leg becomes continuously swollen, hot, red, pale, or cold, or if chest pain or shortness of breath develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; underlying electrolyte imbalances may be present and require routine blood tests.
Clinical Course: Muscular ache will resolve within 24 hours. Cramps may be recurrent but are inherently benign.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical care if the pain becomes constant and does not resolve with stretching, or if the leg changes color to red, pale, or blue.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Cramps are involuntary muscle contractions often related to fatigue or minor electrolyte shifts. Stretching breaks the spasm cycle.
Actionable Steps: Drink adequate water daily. Stretch calves nightly.

